      usedToTreat: "Long-term suppression of inflammatory arthritis and selected autoimmune disease. Methotrexate is a common anchor for rheumatoid arthritis; hydroxychloroquine is useful in milder rheumatoid disease and lupus; leflunomide suppresses activated lymphocyte pyrimidine synthesis; sulfasalazine is used in rheumatoid arthritis and inflammatory bowel disease. These drugs modify disease over weeks to months and do not provide immediate analgesia.",
      description: "Conventional synthetic DMARDs slow immune-mediated tissue injury through different intracellular routes. Low-dose methotrexate increases anti-inflammatory adenosine signaling after intracellular polyglutamation. Hydroxychloroquine accumulates in acidic endosomes and lysosomes, altering antigen processing and nucleic-acid sensing. Leflunomide becomes teriflunomide, which blocks de novo pyrimidine production needed by rapidly activated lymphocytes. Colonic bacteria split sulfasalazine into sulfapyridine and 5-aminosalicylic acid; systemic sulfapyridine is important to rheumatoid benefit while 5-ASA acts mainly in the colon. Because the mechanisms, time courses, organ toxicities, and pregnancy implications differ, the drugs are not interchangeable entries under a generic DMARD label.",
      mechanism: "Methotrexate polyglutamates persist inside cells and inhibit folate-dependent enzymes including ATIC; AICAR accumulates, extracellular adenosine rises, and A2A-receptor signaling suppresses neutrophil and macrophage inflammation. Hydroxychloroquine is a lysosomotropic weak base that raises endosomal pH and reduces TLR7/TLR9 nucleic-acid signaling, antigen processing, and downstream interferon/cytokine activation. Teriflunomide reversibly inhibits mitochondrial dihydroorotate dehydrogenase, limiting de novo uridine synthesis and slowing activated T- and B-cell proliferation. Sulfasalazine is azo-cleaved in the colon; its exact rheumatoid mechanism is not singular, but sulfapyridine exposure alters prostaglandin, cytokine, NF-kappaB, and immune-cell signaling. These distinct pathways explain why toxicity monitoring must follow the molecule rather than the word DMARD.",
