      description: "TNF-alpha is a master inflammatory cytokine that activates endothelial adhesion, leukocyte recruitment, fever, acute-phase signaling, synovial destruction, and granuloma maintenance. Adalimumab and golimumab are fully human monoclonal antibodies; infliximab is a chimeric monoclonal antibody given intravenously; etanercept is a soluble TNF-receptor-2 Fc fusion that also traps lymphotoxin-alpha; certolizumab is a PEGylated antigen-binding fragment without an Fc region. The structural differences matter: because infliximab contains murine variable-region sequences and is infused, antidrug antibodies and infusion reactions deserve special attention. Because etanercept is a receptor fusion rather than an interchangeable monoclonal antibody, its bowel-disease evidence differs. Because certolizumab lacks an Fc region, it lacks FcRn-mediated active placental transport and Fc-dependent effector activity.",
      mechanism: "Soluble and transmembrane TNF-alpha normally bind TNFR1 and TNFR2, recruiting adaptor proteins that activate NF-kappaB, MAP kinase, cell-survival, inflammatory, and context-dependent death pathways. Monoclonal antibodies neutralize TNF and can engage transmembrane TNF; intact IgG1 Fc regions may recruit complement or Fc-receptor effects. Etanercept presents two extracellular TNFR2 domains fused to human IgG1 Fc, functioning as a decoy receptor for TNF-alpha and lymphotoxin-alpha. Certolizumab is a PEGylated Fab prime fragment: it neutralizes TNF but lacks Fc-mediated complement, antibody-dependent cytotoxicity, and active FcRn placental transport. Blocking TNF reduces inflammation but also weakens granuloma integrity, which explains tuberculosis and endemic-fungal reactivation risk. Therefore, a shared target predicts a shared infection warning but does not erase molecule-specific route, Fc, pregnancy-transfer, immunogenicity, or indication differences.",
