      adverseEffects: ["Allopurinol: rash, severe cutaneous reaction, DRESS, liver or kidney injury, cytopenias; febuxostat: cardiovascular death warning, liver abnormalities, rash", "Probenecid: nephrolithiasis, renal colic, hematuria, GI effects, hypersensitivity, and clinically important organic-anion transport interactions"],
      contraindications: ["Never ignore a new allopurinol rash. Clarify HLA-B*58:01 risk, renal function, cardiovascular disease, stones, urine flow, liver tests, pregnancy, and azathioprine/mercaptopurine use.", "Allopurinol and febuxostat can dangerously raise thiopurine exposure because xanthine oxidase participates in mercaptopurine metabolism; the combination requires specialist dose redesign or avoidance."],
      nursingEssentials: ["Explain that early flares do not mean treatment failed. Continue the preventive agent during a flare unless a true toxicity requires stopping, and treat the inflammation separately.", "Trend serum urate and titrate to target while monitoring adherence, kidney/liver safety, flare frequency, tophi, and prophylaxis duration."],
      interactions: ["Allopurinol or febuxostat with azathioprine/mercaptopurine can cause profound myelosuppression; febuxostat labeling contraindicates the combination.", "Probenecid raises concentrations of penicillins, methotrexate, and other organic acids; salicylates antagonize its uricosuric effect."],
      keyLabs: ["Serial serum urate, renal and liver function, CBC when toxicity or thiopurine interaction is relevant, and HLA-B*58:01 before allopurinol in recommended populations", "Flare count, tophus burden, cardiovascular risk, hydration, stone symptoms, urine findings, and medication adherence"],
      nclexTraps: ["A flare after starting urate lowering can reflect crystal mobilization, not rising urate or treatment failure.", "Allopurinol treats chronic urate burden; it does not directly turn off an established acute inflammatory flare."],
      sourceNote: "Current U.S. allopurinol, ULORIC, and probenecid labels plus 2020 ACR gout guideline: https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/lookup.cfm?setid=fc7973c8-d846-4eca-ab0d-d30228035ba2 ; https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/lookup.cfm?setid=54de10ef-fe5f-4930-b91d-6bbb04c664bd ; https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=f2375d90-4132-4fb6-aee7-c4f9d11e98d5 ; https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10563586/",
