      description: "Allopurinol is the preferred first-line long-term urate-lowering drug for most patients with gout, including many with chronic kidney disease. It inhibits xanthine oxidase, the enzyme that converts hypoxanthine to xanthine and xanthine to uric acid. Its active metabolite oxypurinol persists much longer and supplies most of the sustained effect. Lower serum urate allows existing monosodium urate crystals to dissolve gradually, shrinking the tissue reservoir that causes flares, tophi, and urate injury. The paradox is that beginning therapy can temporarily provoke a flare: as serum urate falls, the surface of old deposits remodels and sheds inflammatory microcrystals. That is why allopurinol is started low, titrated to a serum-urate target, paired with temporary flare prophylaxis when appropriate, and continued during an acute flare rather than stopped.",
      mechanism: "Purine catabolism converts hypoxanthine to xanthine and then xanthine to uric acid through xanthine oxidoreductase, usually called xanthine oxidase clinically. Allopurinol resembles hypoxanthine and is converted by the enzyme to oxypurinol; both inhibit xanthine oxidase, with oxypurinol providing prolonged inhibition because renal elimination is slow. Hypoxanthine and xanthine are more soluble than uric acid and can be salvaged back into nucleotides, so net urate production falls. Sustained serum urate below its saturation threshold creates a concentration gradient out of tissue deposits. Kidney function determines oxypurinol exposure, but renal disease is a reason for low starting dose and careful titration, not automatic denial of treat-to-target therapy. The HLA-B*58:01 allele strongly raises the risk of allopurinol hypersensitivity syndrome, SJS/TEN, and DRESS in genetically at-risk populations.",
      boxedWarning: noBoxed("Allopurinol can cause severe and sometimes fatal hypersensitivity, including DRESS, Stevens-Johnson syndrome, toxic epidermal necrolysis, vasculitis, hepatitis, and renal injury. Stop immediately at the first rash, blister, mucosal lesion, facial swelling, fever, eosinophilia, or systemic symptom; do not casually rechallenge. Consider HLA-B*58:01 testing before initiation in genetically high-risk populations. Start lower with kidney impairment and titrate by serum urate. Monitor liver, kidney, and blood counts. Xanthine-oxidase inhibition dangerously increases azathioprine and mercaptopurine exposure unless those doses are drastically reduced under specialist management."),
