      contraindications: ["Serious prior allopurinol hypersensitivity; a positive HLA-B*58:01 result generally makes use inadvisable unless exceptional benefit outweighs risk.", "Clarify ancestry/genetic risk, CKD, liver disease, rash history, thiazides, ampicillin/amoxicillin, azathioprine/mercaptopurine, cancer therapy, pregnancy, and ability to complete titration and monitoring."],
      nursingEssentials: ["Obtain serum urate, CBC, renal and liver tests before treatment; start low, titrate to target rather than a fixed renal ceiling, and provide indicated anti-inflammatory flare prophylaxis.", "Teach that an early flare does not mean failure or allergy: continue urate-lowering therapy and treat the flare, but stop immediately for rash or systemic hypersensitivity signs."],
      interactions: ["Allopurinol blocks metabolism of azathioprine and 6-mercaptopurine, so unadjusted coadministration can cause life-threatening marrow suppression; specialist dose reduction and CBC monitoring are essential.", "Thiazides and renal impairment can increase hypersensitivity risk; ampicillin/amoxicillin increase rash frequency, warfarin effect may change, and didanosine exposure rises."],
      keyLabs: ["Serum urate with a usual gout target below 6 mg/dL, CBC, creatinine/eGFR, AST/ALT, alkaline phosphatase and bilirubin; HLA-B*58:01 when indicated", "Rash/mucosal and systemic hypersensitivity assessment, flare frequency, tophus burden, stone symptoms, adherence, and interacting medicines"],
      nclexTraps: ["Allopurinol prevents future crystal disease by lowering urate; it is not the rapid pain-relief drug for today's flare.", "Do not stop established allopurinol during a flare. Stopping makes urate fluctuate and undermines crystal dissolution; treat the inflammation separately."],
