      description: "Febuxostat is a nonpurine xanthine oxidase inhibitor that lowers uric-acid production without being converted into a purine-like active metabolite. It binds a channel in xanthine oxidase and inhibits both oxidized and reduced forms of the enzyme, lowering conversion of hypoxanthine and xanthine to urate. Like allopurinol, sustained urate reduction permits crystal deposits to dissolve and can initially mobilize crystals enough to trigger flares, so prophylaxis and continued therapy matter. Its major differentiator is safety positioning: in gout patients with established cardiovascular disease, a large outcomes trial found more cardiovascular deaths than with allopurinol. The boxed warning therefore reserves febuxostat for patients in whom adequately titrated allopurinol failed, was not tolerated, or is not advisable.",
      mechanism: "Xanthine oxidoreductase catalyzes the final two steps of purine degradation: hypoxanthine to xanthine and xanthine to uric acid. Febuxostat is a nonpurine inhibitor that occupies the enzyme's molybdenum-pterin access channel and inhibits both oxidized and reduced xanthine oxidase. Uric-acid production falls while more soluble upstream purines rise. The drug is metabolized mainly by conjugation with contributions from CYP1A2, CYP2C8, and CYP2C9, so it is less dependent on renal excretion than oxypurinol but still needs renal and hepatic dose review. Because xanthine oxidase also metabolizes azathioprine and mercaptopurine, febuxostat can cause extreme accumulation and fatal marrow toxicity when combined; that interaction is a contraindication, not a minor dose-timing issue.",
